WEBVTT

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:24.440
<v Ira Glass>The people on 118th Street in New York City are doing this thing that almost nobody in New York or around the country is doing. And to figure out why-- what's it about their block-- I asked a longtime resident, Ivette Rodriguez, to tell me about the place.
<v Ira Glass>纽约市118街的居民们正在做一件纽约乃至全国几乎无人做的事。为了弄清楚原因——也就是这条街有什么特别之处——我请了一位长期住户伊薇特·罗德里格斯（Ivette Rodriguez）为我介绍这里。

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:43.310
<v Ira Glass>And she told me that, when she and her mom moved to the block back in 1965-- this is the very neatly-kept 400 block of East 118th Street, with brick houses and low iron fences where the property line hits the sidewalk. Back when she moved here, the south side of the street was mostly Latino. Her side of the street, the north side, was mostly Italian.
<v Ira Glass>她告诉我，1965年她和她妈妈搬到这条街时——这是东118街400号街区，非常整洁，有砖房，物业线与人行道相接处有低矮的铁栅栏。当时，街道南侧住的大多是拉美裔。她所在的一侧，即北侧，住的大多是意大利裔。

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:57.610
<v Ivette Rodriguez>And we got this apartment because I told my mother, don't say a word. Let me talk. Because if they would have found out that we were Puerto Rican, they would not have rented to us. So I just kept really quiet. She kept quiet.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>我们能租到这套公寓是因为我告诉妈妈，别出声。让我来说。因为如果他们发现我们是波多黎各人，他们就不会租给我们了。所以我一直保持安静。她也保持安静。

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.180
<v Ira Glass>So you guys were passing as Italian.
<v Ira Glass>所以你们假装是意大利人。

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.490
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>是的。是的。

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:20.620
<v Ira Glass>Her mom now owns the building. These days, Ivette says most of the old Italians are gone, and the block is a mix of Latino, white, and Black. There are lots of retirees-- people who arrived decades ago, like Ivette. And younger people are moving in. So we're talking, and I glance at the clock in her apartment and realize the time.
<v Ira Glass>她妈妈现在拥有这栋楼。如今，伊薇特说大多数老意大利人都搬走了，这条街变成了拉美裔、白人和黑人的混合社区。这里有很多退休人员——像伊薇特一样几十年前就搬来的人。也有年轻人搬进来。正聊着，我瞥了一眼她公寓里的时钟，意识到了时间。

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.430
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Don't worry. My husband has an alarm.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>别担心。我丈夫设了闹钟。

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.150
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, no. We're just at two minutes before 7:00 now.
<v Ira Glass>没，不，我们现在离7点还有两分钟。

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.220
<v Ivette Rodriguez>No, this is 10 minutes fast, just in case.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>不，这表快了10分钟，以防万一。

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:28.690
<v Ira Glass>Oh, it is?
<v Ira Glass>哦，是吗？

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:28.950
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Yeah.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>是的。

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.670
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:54.570
<v Ira Glass>I was worried about the time because I was there to record something that starts at 7:00. You remember, early in the pandemic, how people would lean out their windows, or go out at 7:00 PM and cheer for the healthcare workers and other essential workers? OK, most blocks around New York City, most blocks everywhere, stopped doing that long ago. But for some reason, not this block. They stayed at it for over 400 days, everyday.
<v Ira Glass>我担心时间是因为我是来记录7点发生的事情的。记得在疫情初期，人们如何探出窗外，或者在晚上7点出门为医护人员和其他关键岗位工作者欢呼吗？好吧，纽约市的大多数街区，到处的大多数街区，早就停止了这种做法。但不知为何，这条街没有。他们坚持了400多天，每天如此。

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.360
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Yeah, we've got to get ready, Ira.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>好了，艾拉，我们要准备一下了。

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.310
<v Ira Glass>OK. What do we do to get ready?
<v Ira Glass>好的。我们要怎么准备？

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.020
<v Ivette Rodriguez>I've got to put on my jacket--
<v Ivette Rodriguez>我要穿上夹克——

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.070
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.930
<v Ivette Rodriguez>--because I stay out there a long time.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>——因为我要在那儿待很长时间。

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:09.860
<v Ira Glass>Soon enough, at 7:00, we head outside, where some very prompt neighbors have already gathered with pans and noisemakers they use every night.
<v Ira Glass>很快，到了7点，我们走到外面，一些非常准时的邻居已经带着他们每晚使用的平底锅和噪音制造器聚集在那里了。

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.180
<v Ira Glass>[CLANGING]
<v Ira Glass>[金属敲击声]

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.170
<v Ira Glass>[CHEERING]
<v Ira Glass>[欢呼声]

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.670
<v Ira Glass>[CHEERING]
<v Ira Glass>[欢呼声]

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:34.740
<v Ira Glass>Want to guess at how many people it takes to make this much noise? OK, get a number in mind. Eight. From windows in the apartment across the street, some kids wave flashlights. Standing just inside her fence is one of the neighbors who comes out to watch this every night, Frances Mastrota, 83 years old.
<v Ira Glass>猜猜要多少人才能制造出这么大的噪音？好吧，心里想个数字。八个。在街对面公寓的窗户里，有些孩子挥舞着手电筒。站在栅栏内的是每晚都出来观看的邻居之一，83岁的弗朗西斯·马斯特罗塔（Frances Mastrota）。

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:50.190
<v Frances Mastrota>I came on this block in 1959 as a bride. I've been a widow since 1975. And I have a granddaughter that is the class of 2019 of Harvard and now is in Chicago.
<v Frances Mastrota>我1959年作为新娘来到这条街。从1975年起我就守寡了。我有一个孙女是哈佛大学2019届毕业生，现在在芝加哥。

00:02:50.190 --> 00:03:02.050
<v Ira Glass>After Frances introduced herself to me in such a deeply grandmotherly way, I actually had to ask a bunch of questions to learn that Frances is Dr. Frances Mastrota, retired oncology researcher, happy to tell me about her work.
<v Ira Glass>在弗朗西斯以一种极其慈祥的方式自我介绍后，我不得不问了一堆问题，才得知弗朗西斯是弗朗西斯·马斯特罗塔博士，退休的肿瘤学研究员，很乐意向我介绍她的工作。

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:11.940
<v Frances Mastrota>Then as we got better and better, we began to do HLA, Human Leukocyte Antigens, and then look at the genetic code. I'm an old bird.
<v Frances Mastrota>后来随着我们越来越精进，我们开始研究HLA，即人类白细胞抗原，然后观察基因密码。我这老鸟也懂些。

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:32.400
<v Ira Glass>In fact, this is one of the reasons that this block is still doing the 7:00 appreciation of healthcare workers. A bunch of them on the block worked in healthcare, including Frances, and the neighbor who organized the nightly noise party, Vivian, and Ivette, and Ivette's husband. But there are bigger reasons, I think, that this has continued here every night with all the retirees on the block.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，这也是这条街至今仍保持晚上7点向医护人员致敬的原因之一。街上很多人都在医疗领域工作，包括弗朗西斯，还有组织每晚噪音聚会的邻居薇薇安（Vivian），以及伊薇特和伊薇特的丈夫。但我认为，这条街上的退休人员每晚坚持这样做还有更深层的原因。

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:41.920
<v Frances Mastrota>Because we are a very special block, and we watch out for each other. If they don't see I come out at 7:00, they look for me.
<v Frances Mastrota>因为我们是一个非常特别的街区，我们相互照应。如果他们没看到我7点出来，就会找我。

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.740
<v Ira Glass>So is one of the reasons why it's still going on because people are checking on each other?
<v Ira Glass>那么，这还在继续的原因之一是因为人们在互相检查吗？

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:52.440
<v Frances Mastrota>If I don't come out, this lady comes, that lady comes, the people over there come.
<v Frances Mastrota>如果我不出来，这位女士会来，那位女士会来，那边的人也会来。

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.310
<v Ira Glass>Someone will knock on your door to see how you are?
<v Ira Glass>会有人敲你的门看你怎么样吗？

00:03:54.310 --> 00:04:02.820
<v Frances Mastrota>If I don't come out at 7:00, if I don't pick up my New York Times paper at 6:00 they look for me. They know I'm alone.
<v Frances Mastrota>如果我7点没出来，如果我6点没去取《纽约时报》，他们就会找我。他们知道我一个人住。

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.850
<v Ira Glass>Some nights, do you just feel tired, and you don't want to come out?
<v Ira Glass>有些晚上，你感到累了，不想出来吗？

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:10.310
<v Frances Mastrota>I have to. I have to. They will come here! [CHUCKLES]
<v Frances Mastrota>我必须出来。我必须出来。他们会找上门的！[轻笑]

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:21.630
<v Ira Glass>In fact, Ivette and Vivian, who organized all this, do use it to keep an eye out for their neighbors to make sure they're doing OK. And when things quieted down, Ivette ran through her little mental checklist of neighbors for me.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，组织这一切的伊薇特和薇薇安确实利用这个机会留意邻居，确保他们安然无恙。当周围安静下来后，伊薇特为我列出了她心里的邻居清单。

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:34.350
<v Ivette Rodriguez>I saw Mastrota. I saw Severina. Paul came out from next door. That's Sugar. Beverly is here. Arlene is the one that I didn't see.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>我看到马斯特罗塔了。我看到塞韦里纳（Severina）了。保罗（Paul）从隔壁出来了。那是舒格（Sugar）。贝弗利（Beverly）也在这。阿琳（Arlene）是我没看见的。

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:44.580
<v Ira Glass>One neighbor who Ivette checks on every night is her next-door neighbor Josephine, who got COVID and went to the hospital for a while. She's back at home now, but still bedridden, so she doesn't come outside.
<v Ira Glass>伊薇特每晚都会查看的一位邻居是她的隔壁邻居约瑟芬（Josephine），她得了新冠并住了一阵子院。她现在回家了，但仍卧床不起，所以她没法出来。

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.350
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Josephine waved to the window, so she's not angry at me today. [CHUCKLES]
<v Ivette Rodriguez>约瑟芬朝窗户挥手了，所以她今天没生我的气。[轻笑]

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:50.640
<v Ira Glass>And which window was that?
<v Ira Glass>那是哪扇窗户？

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:58.340
<v Ivette Rodriguez>This window right here. It's hard to see. I know because I know what I'm looking for. But I can see her when she waves.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>就是这扇窗户。很难看清。我知道是因为我知道我在找什么。但我能看到她挥手。

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.000
<v Ira Glass>Oh, she doesn't even raise the drape.
<v Ira Glass>哦，她连窗帘都没拉开。

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.600
<v Ivette Rodriguez>No, no.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>不，不。

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.550
<v Ira Glass>You're seeing her through the drape.
<v Ira Glass>你是透过窗帘看到她的。

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.300
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Right.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>没错。

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:03.800
<v Ira Glass>Oh, that's very--
<v Ira Glass>哦，那真是——

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:04.950
<v Ivette Rodriguez>[CHUCKLES]
<v Ivette Rodriguez>[轻笑]

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.550
<v Ira Glass>You really have to know the neighborhood for that.
<v Ira Glass>你确实得非常了解这个街区才能做到。

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:09.570
<v Ivette Rodriguez>Yeah, yeah, yeah.
<v Ivette Rodriguez>是啊，是啊，是啊。

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:20.430
<v Ira Glass>It's just starting to get dark. A pretty night. And people hung out on the sidewalk, chatting in front of Monica Lehmann Gonzalez's house. She's a school teacher and the mother of three school kids.
<v Ira Glass>天刚开始黑。美丽的夜晚。人们在人行道上闲逛，在莫妮卡·莱曼·冈萨雷斯（Monica Lehmann Gonzalez）的家门前聊天。她是一名教师，也是三个上学孩子的母亲。

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:30.040
<v Monica Lehmann Gonzalez>I think the beauty of this is that after all the banging is done, that there's conversations where we just linger, and talk, and kind of catch up. And I think that's so nice.
<v Monica Lehmann Gonzalez>我觉得这最美妙的地方在于，敲击声结束后，大家会留下来聊天，联络感情。我认为这非常好。

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.270
<v Ira Glass>And what will be the sign that the pandemic has gone away enough that you can stop?
<v Ira Glass>那么，什么迹象会表明疫情已经过去，你们可以停下来了？

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:38.610
<v Woman>We have no idea. We really have no idea.
<v Woman>我们不知道。我们真的不知道。

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.460
<v Monica Lehman Gonzalez>I kind of don't want it to end, though. I don't want it to end.
<v Monica Lehman Gonzalez>不过，我倒有点不希望它结束。我不希望它结束。

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.120
<v Ira Glass>Now I'm imagining, COVID is going to end, and you guys are just going to be coming out after COVID.
<v Ira Glass>我现在在想，新冠终会结束，而你们这些家伙即便在新冠之后还会继续出来。

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.440
<v Woman>I think that's a possibility. I think it's a possibility.
<v Woman>我觉得有可能。我觉得有可能。

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:56.030
<v Woman>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Woman>[音乐播放]

00:05:56.030 --> 00:06:11.420
<v Ira Glass>It's the dailiness of the 7:00 get-together, the fact that it happens every single day. That's what's made it mean so much to all of them. They made this part of the day a little life raft that they gathered on during this terrible, dangerous last year that made it like a daily prayer.
<v Ira Glass>是每晚7点聚会的日常性，它每天都发生，这一点让它对他们每个人都意义重大。他们把这一天中的时刻当作在这一年可怕、危险的日子里的一只小救生筏，这让它变得像每天的祈祷一样。

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:30.200
<v Ira Glass>I personally haven't prayed every day since I was a little boy. But somebody who does it as an adult told me that it's the fact that the words and rituals never change day to day that gives comfort. He has days when the prayers mean less to him and days when they mean a lot more. And feeling that difference from day to day also tells him something.
<v Ira Glass>我个人从童年起就没再每天祈祷过。但一位成年后坚持祈祷的人告诉我，正是因为言语和仪式日复一日从未改变，才给予了他慰藉。他有时觉得祈祷对他意义不大，有时觉得意义重大。而这种日复一日的感受差异也给了他某种启示。

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:52.770
<v Ira Glass>Today, we're devoting our program to other things that we do whose dailiness is what is important about them and gives them meaning, helps us see things more clearly. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们将节目致力于那些我们所做的、因其“日常性”而变得重要且赋予意义的事情，它们帮助我们更清晰地看待事物。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:06:52.770 --> 00:07:12.350
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "How Do You Get To Carnegie Hall?" So usually, if we do something every day, like exercise, or floss, or keep a diary, it's because we decide we're going to do it. But other times, it's because the world has chosen for us. This first story is a little bit of both of those. Ben Calhoun tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“如何去卡内基音乐厅？”通常，如果我们每天做某事，比如锻炼、用牙线清洁或写日记，是因为我们决定要这样做。但有时，是因为世界为我们做了选择。这第一个故事兼具这两者。本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun）讲述了发生的事情。

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:25.440
<v Ben Calhoun>The way this story started, Lindsay was in the middle of changing her whole life around. She'd been living and working in Ireland, but she decided to take a leap. She was going to start a new chapter-- move to the other side of the world.
<v Ben Calhoun>故事的开始是这样的，琳赛（Lindsay）当时正处在彻底改变自己生活的过程中。她一直住在爱尔兰工作，但她决定放手一搏。她要开启新篇章——搬到地球的另一端。

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:32.660
<v Lindsay>I had actually just accepted a job, and I was meant to move to Australia in April.
<v Lindsay>我当时其实刚接受了一份工作，本打算四月份搬到澳大利亚。

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:36.630
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, so you were getting ready to leave Dublin. You were on your way out the door.
<v Ben Calhoun>哦，所以你当时正准备离开都柏林。你正准备出门。

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:54.870
<v Lindsay>Totally. I was actually almost all the way packed, truly ready to move. And I was dating an Australian guy who had just moved to Australia. And I was meant to follow him a couple weeks later. And then the pandemic hit.
<v Lindsay>完全是这样。我当时行李几乎都装好了，真心准备搬家。而且我当时正在和一个刚搬到澳大利亚的澳大利亚男生约会。我本打算几周后跟随他而去。然后疫情爆发了。

00:07:54.870 --> 00:08:21.340
<v Ben Calhoun>Lindsay couldn't leave, and she had to scramble to find a place to stay. Eventually, she got an apartment on the south side of Dublin. It was actually this great place-- a one-bedroom in a really pretty row house. It looks like it was pulled out of 101 Dalmatians or something. The place was cozy. It had these pretty bay windows that looked out onto the street and a sunny little breakfast nook in the kitchen-- Not that things were going super great for Lindsay otherwise.
<v Ben Calhoun>琳赛无法离开，她不得不争分夺秒地寻找住所。最终，她在都柏林南侧租到了一间公寓。那其实是个很棒的地方——一间带真正漂亮排屋的一居室。看起来就像是从《101忠狗》或类似电影里搬出来的一样。那个地方很温馨。它有漂亮的凸窗，可以看到街道，厨房里还有一个阳光明媚的小早餐角——但这并不意味着琳赛的生活在那之后就一帆风顺了。

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:43.480
<v Ben Calhoun>She and that Australian guy broke up. And her move, her new job, new life-- all that got called off in slow motion. And Lindsay, she was alone in lockdown. She says this was actually one of the more depressing times she remembers. And it was in the middle of all of that that she started to hear music three or four times a day.
<v Ben Calhoun>她和那个澳大利亚男生分手了。她的搬家、新工作、新生活——这一切都以慢动作告吹。琳赛被独自困在封锁中。她说这是她记忆中最令人沮丧的时光之一。就在这一切之中，她开始每天三四次听到音乐。

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:54.120
<v Lindsay>I would notice when I was-- I'd be doing the dishes, or I'd be doing something so mundane. And then it would happen. Yeah.
<v Lindsay>我会注意到当我——我在洗碗时，或者在做些琐事时。然后它就发生了。是的。

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:14.420
<v Ben Calhoun>The music was coming from nearby. It was a saxophone, somebody practicing, pretty clearly a beginner. But the most notable part of it was the song. It was the same one everyday, again, and again, and again. It was the Pink Panther theme song.
<v Ben Calhoun>音乐从附近传来。那是一把萨克斯风，有人在练习，很明显是个初学者。但最引人注目的是那首歌。每天都是同一首，一遍又一遍，一遍又一遍。那是《粉红豹》的主题曲。

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:22.180
<v Ben Calhoun>[SAXOPHONE PLAYING]
<v Ben Calhoun>[萨克斯风演奏]

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:49.240
<v Ben Calhoun>What you're hearing is actually the sound of a video montage Lindsay put together. And the funny of this was not lost on her. In the video, you notice the range of time stamps-- morning, afternoon, 10:30 at night, "The Pink Panther Theme" always in the background. You'll hear it as Lindsay eats at her kitchen table or leans against the door frame in her living room, holding back a smile, as she lays on her yoga mat or walks through her hall.
<v Ben Calhoun>你现在听到的是琳赛制作的视频蒙太奇的声音。其中的滑稽之处琳赛心知肚明。在视频中，你注意到时间戳的跨度——早上、下午、晚上10点半，《粉红豹》主题曲始终在背景中播放。你会听到它伴随着琳赛在餐桌吃东西，或者倚在客厅门框上强忍着笑容，或者躺在瑜伽垫上，或者走在走廊里。

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:54.940
<v Ben Calhoun>When he picked up the horn, and you first heard the notes come out, you were like, oh, I'm in for how long?
<v Ben Calhoun>当他拿起萨克斯，你第一次听到音符出来时，你当时的反应是，哦，我要经历多久？

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.790
<v Lindsay>I'd say 45 minutes or an hour.
<v Lindsay>我会说45分钟到一个小时。

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.530
<v Ben Calhoun>45 minutes to an hour? Wow.
<v Ben Calhoun>45分钟到一个小时？哇。

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:06.670
<v Lindsay>Or maybe-- oh, god, now I feel like, is that crazy? Maybe 20 to 30 minutes?
<v Lindsay>或者也许——天哪，我现在觉得，那是不是疯了？也许20到30分钟？

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.410
<v Ben Calhoun>No, I mean--
<v Ben Calhoun>不，我是说——

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:16.720
<v Lindsay>Maybe it was 20 to 30 minutes. It felt, to me, like it was never ending. It felt, to me, by the end, that I was just-- it was all day, all night.
<v Lindsay>也许是20到30分钟。对我来说，感觉它没完没了。最后给我的感觉是，我只是——整天整夜都是它。

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:21.310
<v Ben Calhoun>So it became a staple of your existence.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以它成了你生活的一部分。

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:32.360
<v Lindsay>Yeah, and it was to the point where I was trying to work during the day. I was trying to get things done. And then I'd be in a meeting, and all my co-workers could hear.
<v Lindsay>是的，以至于我在白天尝试工作时，我正努力处理事情。然后我正在开会，所有的同事都能听到。

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:44.700
<v Ben Calhoun>Lindsay was now working remotely from this Dublin apartment. And all the Pink Panther-ing became a running joke for the people that she worked with. It was so audible that Lindsay found herself constantly having to mute during video meetings.
<v Ben Calhoun>琳赛现在在都柏林这间公寓远程办公。所有的“粉红豹”曲子成了她同事间的一个老梗。声音大到琳赛发现自己在视频会议时不得不不断静音。

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:54.390
<v Ben Calhoun>Because mics on phones vary so much, I couldn't totally grasp how loud it was in person. How loud was it?
<v Ben Calhoun>因为手机上的麦克风差别很大，我没法完全体会现场有多大声。到底有多大声？

00:10:54.390 --> 00:11:16.830
<v Lindsay>Oh, it sounded like it was in my house. So when I posted this on Twitter, I got all kinds of responses. But people were wondering if I was playing a recording of it in my house. But it was so loud that it did sound like it was coming from inside the house. And I don't know if he knew how loud it was.
<v Lindsay>哦，听起来就像在我屋里一样。所以当我把它发到推特上时，我收到了各种各样的回应。但人们想知道我是否在屋里播放它的录音。但它真的太响了，听起来确实像是从屋子里面传出来的。我不知道他是否知道声音有多大。

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:17.250
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的。

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.110
<v Lindsay>And I don't think he did.
<v Lindsay>我觉得他不知道。

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:47.310
<v Ben Calhoun>Lindsay says this went on for weeks and weeks, several times every day. Just to point out, most days, this was actually her only real-life human contact-- the saxophone coming through the walls. The video montage that Lindsay made of all this, she eventually tweeted it, and it accumulated 5.4 million views. Just for scale, Miles Davis' Kind of Blue has sold 5 million copies.
<v Ben Calhoun>琳赛说这种情况持续了几个星期又几个星期，每天好几次。需要指出的是，大多数日子里，这其实是她唯一真实的现实接触——通过墙壁传来的萨克斯声。琳赛制作的所有这一切的视频蒙太奇，她最终发了推文，积累了540万次观看。作为对比，迈尔斯·戴维斯（Miles Davis）的《Kind of Blue》卖出了500万张。

00:11:47.310 --> 00:12:13.200
<v Ben Calhoun>The internet responded like the internet. Some of the 4.7 thousand comments-- "interesting how they got further from nailing it the longer they practiced." "Your neighbor's horrible playing should be a crime." "My condolences." "You'd think that the performance would at least improve over time." Someone chimes in, "Sounds like he's still shite, too." The insurance company Geico responded, "We feel your pain."
<v Ben Calhoun>互联网的反应正如互联网一贯的表现。在4700条评论中，有些写着：“有意思的是，他们练习得越久，离练好就越远。”“你邻居糟糕的演奏应该被定罪。”“向你表示慰问。”“你本以为表演至少会有所进步。”有人附和道：“听起来他现在还是很烂。”保险公司Geico回应称：“我们感同身受。”

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:27.000
<v Ben Calhoun>For a long time, Lindsay didn't know who the Pink Panther-er was. So when she'd see people in the halls of her building, it was a little like, is that them? Wait, is that them? Though eventually, her suspicions snagged on this one guy.
<v Ben Calhoun>很长一段时间里，琳赛不知道那个“粉红豹”演奏者是谁。所以当她在楼道里看到人时，会有点像，是他们吗？等等，是他们吗？尽管最终，她的怀疑锁定了一个人。

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:38.580
<v Lindsay>I would see this guy outside. He would always do this thing where he would walk outside to drink his coffee in his pajamas. And I had a feeling it was him, but I wasn't sure.
<v Lindsay>我总会看到这个家伙在外面。他总是会做这件事，穿着睡衣走到外面喝咖啡。我有一种感觉就是他，但我不能确定。

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:44.550
<v Ben Calhoun>For some reason, I feel like drinking coffee outside in your pajamas is kind of saxophone behavior.
<v Ben Calhoun>出于某种原因，我觉得穿着睡衣在外面喝咖啡就是一种“萨克斯风行为”。

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.850
<v Lindsay>Yeah, he had a vibe.
<v Lindsay>是的，他有种气场。

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.200
<v Ben Calhoun>It was a saxophone vibe.
<v Ben Calhoun>那是一种萨克斯风气场。

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.160
<v Lindsay>He had a saxophone vibe, for sure.
<v Lindsay>当然有萨克斯风气场。

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:59.510
<v Lindsay>[MUSIC - "THE PINK PANTHER THEME"]
<v Lindsay>[音乐 - 《粉红豹主题曲》]

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:21.170
<v Lindsay>So come with me, if you will, to the other side of Lindsay's wall, to the other end of the saxophone rainbow, where there was a young man named Rajat. At the time, he was 23. Like Lindsay, he was living and working in Ireland. As far as Rajat knew, his only audience was inside his apartment at this window where he would practice.
<v Lindsay>那么，请随我一起，来到琳赛墙的另一边，来到萨克斯风彩虹的另一端，那里有一位名叫拉贾特（Rajat）的年轻人。那时他23岁。像琳赛一样，他在爱尔兰生活工作。就拉贾特所知，他唯一的观众就在他练习窗户的公寓里。

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:36.750
<v Rajat>I had a large window. And on the windowsill, I had three plants, a basil plant-- I just randomly had a potato that was going off, and I put it into a pot, and it started to grow. So that was my potted potato plant. And there was a coriander plant as well.
<v Rajat>我有一扇大窗户。在窗台上，我有三盆植物，一盆罗勒——我随手有一个发芽的土豆，我把它种进盆里，它就开始生长了。所以那是我的盆栽土豆植物。还有一盆香菜。

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:47.810
<v Rajat>So I would play to them. That's my audience every day. And I still believe that that potted potato plant grew, and it took off. So I think I had something to do with it.
<v Rajat>所以我每天都对着它们弹奏。那就是我每天的观众。我至今仍相信那盆土豆植物长得那么高，肯定有我的一份功劳。

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:49.610
<v Rajat>[CHUCKLING]
<v Rajat>[轻笑]

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:53.000
<v Rajat>No joke. It was super tall.
<v Rajat>没开玩笑。它长得超级高。

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:11.120
<v Ben Calhoun>Rajat told me, for the record, he had no idea people could hear him like this. He said, some people in the building had remarked on how he was learning the saxophone. But he just figured, this building is old. It's got to have thick walls. Maybe they hear me faintly, off in the distance.
<v Ben Calhoun>拉贾特告诉我，郑重声明，他根本不知道人们能这样听到他演奏。他说，楼里有些人提到过他在学萨克斯风。但他只是觉得，这栋楼很老。墙一定很厚。也许他们只是隐约听到一点远处的声响。

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:20.600
<v Ben Calhoun>Lindsay did eventually come over at some point. She introduced herself, and she asked him to stop because she had a migraine. Rajat says Lindsay was super nice.
<v Ben Calhoun>琳赛最终确实在那次去了他那。她自我介绍，并要求他停下来，因为她偏头痛。拉贾特说琳赛超级好。

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:37.970
<v Ben Calhoun>Lindsay says Rajat was genuinely surprised and very sweet. For my fellow rom-com fans, I will note that they are not engaged. But the point is, Rajat actually had no idea how loud his saxophone playing was for her until he saw Lindsay's video later.
<v Ben Calhoun>琳赛说拉贾特真的感到惊讶且非常甜。对于我的浪漫喜剧影迷同好们，我要指出他们并没有订婚。但重点是，拉贾特其实根本不知道他的萨克斯演奏对她来说有多大声，直到后来他看到了琳赛的视频。

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:56.230
<v Rajat>When I saw the video, I was like, wow, that's really loud in the house. I did not think it was that loud in her house. Because that was almost how loud it was for me when I was playing. And so when I heard it for the first time from her perspective, I was like, yeah, I would have had a problem as well if I was there.
<v Rajat>当我看到视频时，我当时就想，哇，在屋子里真的太响了。我没想到在她屋子里声音有那么大。因为那几乎就是我在演奏时听到的音量。所以当我第一次从她的角度听到时，我当时就想，是的，如果我在那儿，我也会有意见的。

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:58.300
<v Rajat>[CHUCKLING]
<v Rajat>[轻笑]

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:23.000
<v Ben Calhoun>Rajat felt like, oh, shoot, I did not know I was bothering anyone. He asked Lindsay what would be a good time to play, and he started practicing during his lunch hour, though an hour was barely enough, he told me. Because what was unfolding on Rajat's side of the building was a love story, a budding romance with the alto saxophone that had started just a few months before this.
<v Ben Calhoun>拉贾特觉得，哦，糟糕，我不知道我打扰了别人。他问琳赛什么时候演奏比较好，然后他开始在午休时间练习，尽管他告诉我，一个小时根本不够。因为在拉贾特那一侧发生的，是一个爱情故事，一种与中音萨克斯风萌芽的浪漫情怀，那是在此之前几个月才开始的。

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:39.080
<v Ben Calhoun>Turns out, before COVID hit, in December, Rajat was visiting India. He's from Bangalore. And he was visiting old friends. One of these friends lives on a farm. Rajat says his place is just beautiful. So all these friends, they're there, and they're hanging out.
<v Ben Calhoun>结果是，在疫情爆发前的12月，拉贾特正在访问印度。他来自班加罗尔。他在看望老朋友。其中一个朋友住在农场。拉贾特说他住的地方太美了。所以所有的朋友都在那儿闲逛。

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:49.980
<v Rajat>And one of them picked up a saxophone, right? And it was alto sax, this nice, cute little guy. And like, we were all just-- we were working. And he has a little wood shop. Then we were working.
<v Rajat>然后其中一个人拿起了萨克斯风，对吧？那是中音萨克斯，这个漂亮可爱的小东西。然后，我们都在——我们都在忙活。他有一个小木工车间。我们当时在干活。

00:15:49.980 --> 00:16:12.010
<v Rajat>And from the background, I hear someone playing the beginning riff of "Ain't No Sunshine." And I look out, and it's my friend. He's wearing slippers-- flip-flops and shorts-- sweating because the sun is out, even in December in India. And it's like the Pied Piper. There are a few dogs walking behind him, and he's playing the tune. And I was like, yo, that's amazing. What?
<v Rajat>从背景里，我听到有人在吹《Ain't No Sunshine》的开头即兴段。我向外看，原来是我的朋友。他穿着拖鞋——人字拖和短裤——满头大汗，因为即便在12月的印度，太阳也很大。他就像穿着魔笛手一样。几只狗跟在他后面，他正吹着那曲子。我就想，哟，太棒了。什么情况？

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:24.990
<v Ben Calhoun>Rajat's friend was like, hey, you should try it. He was like, OK. Almost immediately, he was able to get a sound out of the horn. So his friend taught him the first line from "Ain't No Sunshine," and Rajat was off and running.
<v Ben Calhoun>拉贾特的朋友说，嘿，你应该试试。他说，好。几乎立刻，他就能让萨克斯发出声音了。所以他的朋友教了他《Ain't No Sunshine》的第一句，拉贾特就这样开始了。

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:39.630
<v Rajat>And I was just off by myself, playing out into the fields. Literally, I would stay for a good-- if not 45, at least 30 minutes or so, I was just constantly drilling, and drilling, and drilling, and drilling, and drilling it. And that was the moment.
<v Rajat>我就独自一人，对着田野演奏。真的，我会在那儿待上好一会儿——如果不是45分钟，至少30分钟左右，我只是在不停地练习，练习，练习，练习，练习那一段。那正是那一刻。

00:16:39.630 --> 00:17:03.140
<v Rajat>I was getting into a flow. And I think, also, with the saxophone, because you keep blowing so much, there's some degree of-- and then you're like-- [GASPS] --and then you blow again. So there's a high degree of when you rapid breathe when you meditate. There's some degree of you getting kind of like, whoa, what's happening? And so I was getting into this flow state with that. And I was like, wow, this is so good.
<v Rajat>我进入了心流状态。而且我认为，萨克斯风因为你一直在吹气，某种程度上——然后你是这样——[吸气]——然后你再吹。所以当你冥想时快速呼吸，有很大程度的——某种程度你会觉得，哇，发生了什么？所以我进入了那种心流状态。我就觉得，哇，这真是太好了。

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:05.820
<v Rajat>[LAUGHTER]
<v Rajat>[笑声]

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:16.740
<v Ben Calhoun>Getting that sound to come out of the saxophone right away, did you feel a little bit like, oh, I'm kind of a natural?
<v Ben Calhoun>让萨克斯风立刻发出那个声音，你有没有感觉，哦，我有点天赋？

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.710
<v Rajat>For sure, yeah. Yeah, yeah, for sure.
<v Rajat>当然，是的。是的，是的，当然。

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:28.710
<v Ben Calhoun>When he got back to Ireland, he researched carefully. And he bought a saxophone, an alto. And then he set about learning to play it.
<v Ben Calhoun>当他回到爱尔兰后，他仔细研究了一番。然后他买了一把中音萨克斯。然后他开始学习演奏它。

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:59.220
<v Ben Calhoun>He made this little notebook for himself. On the first page-- all caps-- he wrote, "CONSISTENCY IS KEY," meaning, practice everyday, a little bit, or a lot. On the second page, he made a list of songs that he wanted to learn, including, of course, "The Pink Panther." Rajat told me this was the song he maybe most identified with the saxophone. And so he found a video tutorial of it online, where a guy walks through the song phrase by phrase.
<v Ben Calhoun>他为自己做了个小笔记本。在第一页——全大写——他写着，“持之以恒是关键”，意思是，每天练习，一点点或者多一点。在第二页，他列出了想学的歌单，当然包括《粉红豹》。拉贾特告诉我，这可能是他最能与萨克斯风产生共鸣的曲子。所以他在网上找到了一个视频教程，里面有人逐句地讲解这首歌。

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:08.520
<v Rajat>And so I would have drilled that video 80-plus times, just hard, just again, and again, and again, and again. And just keep going, keep going, keep going.
<v Rajat>所以我把那个视频练习了80遍以上，只是刻苦练习，一遍，又一遍，又一遍，又一遍。一直继续，一直继续，一直继续。

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.520
<v Ben Calhoun>You're like, I'm going to get this into my muscle memory.
<v Ben Calhoun>你当时就想，我要把它练进肌肉记忆里。

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:25.220
<v Rajat>Yeah, exactly. And because it was something I really wanted to get good, because it sounded so cool, the final product at the end of that video, that I was like, I need to get this. And I was not going to move on to anything else until I got this done.
<v Rajat>是的，完全正确。而且因为那是我真心想练好的东西，因为它听起来太酷了，视频最后的成品，所以我当时想，我一定要学会它。如果没学会，我绝不会进行下一项。

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:26.100
<v Rajat>[LAUGHTER]
<v Rajat>[笑声]

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:31.120
<v Rajat>So at one point in time, it was not in heavy rotation. It was the rotation, you know?
<v Rajat>所以一度，它不在轮播列表里。它就是唯一的轮播内容，你知道吗？

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:35.070
<v Rajat>[LAUGHTER]
<v Rajat>[笑声]

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:48.460
<v Rajat>Yeah, but I was not getting tired of it. It was entertaining. And it was so nice to see. Because at the end of it as well, there's this beautiful riff. [VOCALIZING]
<v Rajat>是的，但我没有感到厌倦。它很有娱乐性。看到它真的很好。因为在曲子最后，有一段优美的即兴段。[哼唱]

00:18:48.460 --> 00:19:13.100
<v Rajat>And oh, that's so cool! It's so funky when you get it right. So it would give me glimpses. I'd do the first three phases horribly, but I'd say, OK, play to the end. And just the one time-- one time-- if I get the nice little riff properly, I'd get like, oh, this is going so well.
<v Rajat>哦，那太酷了！练好后真的很时髦。所以它会给我一些闪光点。我前三个阶段做得一团糟，但我会说，好的，吹到最后。只要那一次——就那一次——如果我能把那段即兴吹好，我会感到，哦，这进行得太顺利了。

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:39.590
<v Ben Calhoun>The thing I love about the situation is just how different the same song played day in and day out for months could feel in these two neighboring apartments by two people in their own little bubbles, like some kind of musical Rorschach test. For Lindsay, whether it was irritating or funny, it was an artifact of her confinement. But for Rajat, it was the exact opposite. It was an escape.
<v Ben Calhoun>关于这种情况我最喜欢的一点是，同一首歌日复一日地在两个相邻的公寓里被两个人以自己的小泡泡播放，竟然能产生如此不同的感觉，就像某种音乐版罗夏墨迹测试。对于琳赛来说，无论它是恼人还是滑稽，它都是她禁足生活的产物。但对于拉贾特来说，恰恰相反。这是一种逃避。

00:19:39.590 --> 00:20:00.160
<v Rajat>Yeah, because there's only so much Netflix you can do until you start feeling sick of doing that. And yeah, so this was genuinely-- I got up. I was like, oh, I'm going to get to practice saxophone today. That got me through, you know?
<v Rajat>是的，因为奈飞看多了你就会开始觉得腻了。所以这真的是——我起床了。我就想，哦，我今天可以练萨克斯风了。这帮我撑过了那段时光，你知道吗？

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:24.710
<v Ben Calhoun>Looking back on this little chapter, for Lindsay's part, she told me, the main consequence of all this has been that, months after leaving that apartment, moving back to the United States, she's still hearing "The Pink Panther Theme" all the time. It's just coming through the walls of her memory now, like a phantom limb, a pink phantom limb. All of this has also stuck with Rajat.
<v Ben Calhoun>回望这个小篇章，对琳赛而言，她告诉我，这一切的主要后果是，在离开那间公寓几个月、搬回美国后，她仍然一直听到《粉红豹》主题曲。它现在就像一个幻肢，一个粉色的幻肢，穿过她记忆的墙壁传过来。这一切也留在了拉贾特心中。

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:36.480
<v Rajat>So believe it or not, but this 5 million view thing has scarred me to a certain extent. Because honestly, I've now moved to Italy, right? I'm staying here.
<v Rajat>所以信不信由你，这个500万观看的视频在某种程度上留下了创伤。因为说实话，我现在已经搬到意大利了，对吧？我住在那儿。

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:47.420
<v Rajat>And I live in these apartments where you share a courtyard. So I have a balcony right here, and it opens out into this beautiful courtyard. You can see the sky, birds on top. Lovely.
<v Rajat>我住的公寓共用一个庭院。我这儿有个阳台，它正对着这个美丽的庭院。你可以看到天空，顶上有鸟。可爱极了。

00:20:47.420 --> 00:21:05.680
<v Rajat>But there's just everybody around here, and it echoes. So I cannot play the saxophone here because, at least in Dublin, if someone had an issue, I could speak to them and try to diffuse the situation. I cannot do that in Italian. [LAUGHS] I can't.
<v Rajat>但周围到处都是人，而且有回声。所以我不能在这儿吹萨克斯，因为至少在都柏林，如果有人有意见，我可以和他们沟通并尝试化解。我没法用意大利语那样做。[笑] 我做不到。

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:22.550
<v Rajat>So I actually found a spot in. You know the storage rooms that are super dank, in the cellar? Everything is musty, where they keep the suitcases. I managed to go there once and clear out some space. And I was like, this will be my saxophone room.
<v Rajat>所以我其实在地窖里发现了一个地方。你知道地窖里那些超级阴暗的储藏室吗？什么东西都发霉，他们存放行李的地方。我设法去过那里一次，清理出一些空间。我当时就想，这会是我的萨克斯练习室。

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:47.460
<v Ben Calhoun>Rajat says, though, it's just so out of the way, he hasn't done it. For Rajat, lockdowning in Dublin, he had really been in this bubble where, true or not, he was so convinced no one could hear him. It was like he got to spend swaths of his day singing in the shower, dancing like no one was watching, loving "The Pink Panther Theme" like he'd never been hurt, if you know what I mean.
<v Ben Calhoun>不过拉贾特说，因为那太偏僻了，他还没去过。对于在都柏林封锁期间的拉贾特来说，他真的处于一个泡泡里，不管真假，他深信没人能听到他。就像他可以在淋浴时唱歌，像没人看着那样跳舞，像从未受过伤一样热爱《粉红豹》主题曲，如果你明白我的意思。

00:21:47.460 --> 00:22:00.690
<v Ben Calhoun>That became his everyday. And since then, well, he's been a little unsure how to get back to that spot where he can play and feel quite so free. He's been chasing that feeling. Aren't we all?
<v Ben Calhoun>那成了他的日常。从那以后，好吧，他有点不知道怎么回到那个可以演奏并感到如此自由的境地了。他一直在追逐那种感觉。我们不都是这样吗？

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:09.410
<v Ben Calhoun>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ben Calhoun>[音乐播放]

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:21.610
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>本·卡尔霍恩是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:38.620
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "So Nice to Hear Your Voice." We now turn to somebody who had one daily routine that changed to a different daily routine. And I don't want to get ahead of the story too much but just to say what a vast effect it had on her and how quickly those effects kicked in.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“听到你的声音真好。”我们现在转向另一位有每日例行公事并转变为另一种例行公事的人。我不打算剧透太多故事，只想说这对她产生了多么巨大的影响，以及这些影响生效得多么迅速。

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:54.190
<v Ira Glass>The person with the daily routine in the story is Dee Brown. She moved into long-term care five years ago. And for four years, somebody would visit her everyday. Family or her best friend would come nearly everyday. They'd go out to eat, go on long drives into the mountains.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事里有每日例行公事的人是迪·布朗（Dee Brown）。她五年前搬进了长期护理机构。四年来，每天都有人来看她。家人或她最好的朋友几乎每天都会来。他们会出去吃饭，去山上做长途驾驶。

00:22:54.190 --> 00:23:03.790
<v Ira Glass>Then COVID hit. And the facilities she was in, following national guidelines, didn't allow anybody to have any visitors. Communal dining and group activities were also cancelled.
<v Ira Glass>然后新冠爆发了。她所在的机构遵循国家指导方针，不允许任何人探视。社区用餐和集体活动也都被取消了。

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:14.980
<v Ira Glass>So Dee's family invented a new routine-- daily phone calls. Basically, they had made a schedule for everybody in the family to phone. And that way, Dee would get calls every single day.
<v Ira Glass>于是迪的家人发明了一种新的日常——每日电话。基本上，他们为家里每个人安排了一个打电话的日程表。这样，迪每天都能接到电话。

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:26.920
<v Ira Glass>They would chat. They would socialize. They would try to keep her spirits up, try to keep her from getting bored. Dee's granddaughter Cecilia Brown was one of the callers, phoning once a week or so. And she put together this story.
<v Ira Glass>他们会聊天。他们会社交。他们努力让她保持精神振作，尽量不让她感到无聊。迪的孙女塞西莉亚·布朗（Cecilia Brown）是打电话的人之一，大约每周打一次。她整理了这个故事。

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:35.660
<v Cecilia Brown>When my family started this, we figured naively, we'd do this for a few weeks, maybe a month, until things got back to normal. And the calls were fun.
<v Cecilia Brown>当我家人开始这样做时，我们天真地以为，我们会这样做几周，也许一个月，直到一切恢复正常。这些电话很有趣。

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:39.010
<v Cecilia Brown>Hello? Dee? Can you hear me?
<v Cecilia Brown>喂？迪？你能听到我吗？

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:42.370
<v Cecilia Brown>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Cecilia Brown>[音乐播放]

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:46.600
<v Ira Glass>My first call, March 27, we were still getting used to the phone.
<v Ira Glass>我的第一次通话，3月27日，我们还在适应电话。

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:48.220
<v Cecilia Brown>Dee, I think you put me on hold.
<v Cecilia Brown>迪，我想你把我设为保持通话了。

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:56.560
<v Ira Glass>But Dee was pretty happy. She'd start most calls by telling me about how nice her apartment was. She took her calls from her favorite lounge chair.
<v Ira Glass>但迪很高兴。她大部分电话开始时都会告诉我她的公寓有多好。她是在她最喜欢的躺椅上接电话的。

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:57.940
<v Dee Brown>Hello?
<v Dee Brown>喂？

00:23:57.940 --> 00:23:59.410
<v Cecilia Brown>Oh, that was weird.
<v Cecilia Brown>哦，那太怪了。

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:03.190
<v Dee Brown>Cecilia, yeah, hello. How are you doing?
<v Dee Brown>塞西莉亚，是的，喂。你最近好吗？

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:05.140
<v Cecilia Brown>I'm good. I'm good.
<v Cecilia Brown>我很好。我很好。

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:06.500
<v Dee Brown>Yeah, what's up?
<v Dee Brown>是啊，怎么了？

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:11.950
<v Cecilia Brown>Dee loved to say the unexpected thing, make fun of herself if it meant making other people laugh.
<v Cecilia Brown>迪喜欢说些意想不到的话，如果能让别人笑出来，她不介意拿自己开玩笑。

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:31.510
<v Dee Brown>Oh, I have a real problem right now. I got some underwear, the kind that stretches, only it's too tight. And I sit down and they go, ugh, my stomach. [LAUGHS] Those are big problems, right?
<v Dee Brown>哦，我现在有个真正的问题。我买了一些内衣，那种有弹性的，结果太紧了。我一坐下来它们就说，额，我的肚子。[笑] 这些都是大问题，对吧？

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:33.500
<v Cecilia Brown>Yeah, I have that problem, too.
<v Cecilia Brown>是的，我也有那个问题。

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:41.140
<v Dee Brown>Yeah, well, you just think of me as you're trying to pull your underwear down so it makes you feel very graceful.
<v Dee Brown>是的，好吧，当你试图把内衣往下拉时，就把我当成你，这样你会觉得很优雅。

00:24:41.140 --> 00:24:42.850
<v Dee Brown>[LAUGHTER]
<v Dee Brown>[笑声]

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:59.710
<v Cecilia Brown>Growing up, Dee wasn't what I thought of as a typical grandma. For one thing, we call her Dee, not grandma. She also dressed like an old Colorado hippie-- tons of turquoise jewelry and tie dye. She always smelled like coffee, which she drank all day, even at midnight.
<v Cecilia Brown>成长过程中，迪不是我想象中那种典型的奶奶。首先，我们叫她迪，而不是奶奶。她的装扮也像老科罗拉多嬉皮士——戴着一堆绿松石首饰，穿扎染衣服。她身上总有咖啡味，她整天喝咖啡，哪怕半夜也喝。

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:12.320
<v Cecilia Brown>She never did the normal grandma stuff with my sister and me, like take us out for ice cream or whatever. Dee was a talker, so that's what we'd do-- sit in the backyard and talk for hours. It didn't matter how old you were.
<v Cecilia Brown>她从不和我和妹妹做那种普通的奶奶做的事，比如带我们出去吃冰淇淋之类的。迪是个健谈的人，所以那就是我们会做的——坐在后院聊上几个小时。不管你多大。

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:33.620
<v Cecilia Brown>And she was funny. Though my dad said she'd sometimes disguise something as a joke, just to get what she wanted. In her 40's, after raising three kids and getting a divorce, she ended up in AA. It became a huge part of her life, so much so she ended up sponsoring over 100 people. She'd still go to meetings a few times a week. Now, of course, all that was gone.
<v Cecilia Brown>而且她很幽默。虽然我爸说她有时会把真心话伪装成笑话，只为了达成目的。40多岁时，在抚养三个孩子并离婚后，她最终参加了匿名戒酒会（AA）。这成了她生命中很大的一部分，以至于她最终资助了100多人。她每周还会去开几次会。现在，当然，这一切都没了。

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:42.230
<v Dee Brown>Yeah, it's been kind of a boring day because I haven't been able to go to the store and call my friend who has my car.
<v Dee Brown>是啊，今天有点无聊，因为我没法去商店，也不能给那个帮我存车的朋友打电话。

00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:45.910
<v Cecilia Brown>Yeah. Can you walk in the hallways?
<v Cecilia Brown>是啊。你能在走廊里走吗？

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:56.410
<v Dee Brown>Yeah. Yeah, I walk through the hallways, and everybody else is walking in the hallway, too, you know? Because they're just trying to get a little exercise.
<v Dee Brown>是的。是的，我在走廊里走，其他人也在走廊里走，你知道吗？因为他们只是想稍微锻炼一下。

00:25:56.410 --> 00:26:07.540
<v Dee Brown>But this will pass. And we'll be OK. It's not going to kill me, sitting in here instead of going out and walking around.
<v Dee Brown>但这都会过去的。我们会没事的。这杀不死我，只是坐在这里而不是出去散步。

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:19.630
<v Cecilia Brown>I taped these calls with her permission. Dee was going on 88. I'd done the same thing with my other grandmother when she hit her late 80's. It was just something to capture a little bit of their lives on tape while I still had them.
<v Cecilia Brown>我在她的允许下录了这些电话。迪快88岁了。我在另一位奶奶快80岁时也做过同样的事。这只是为了在她们还在的时候，录下一点她们的生活。

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:29.140
<v Cecilia Brown>Dee had dementia, but it was pretty mild. She'd forget small things like where I lived, tell me stories she told me before. But she was still pretty with it.
<v Cecilia Brown>迪患有痴呆症，但程度很轻。她会忘记小事，比如我住哪里，跟我讲她讲过的故事。但她思路还算清晰。

00:26:29.140 --> 00:26:40.900
<v Cecilia Brown>This was another reason for the frequent calls. Isolation is really bad for anyone with dementia. You've probably heard people say the brain is like a muscle. You need to use it to maintain it.
<v Cecilia Brown>这也是频繁打电话的另一个原因。隔离对任何痴呆症患者来说都很糟糕。你可能听人说过大脑就像肌肉。你需要使用它来维持它。

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:57.350
<v Cecilia Brown>With dementia, this is especially true. The more you use your mind, use words, the more those things stick with you. But when you're isolated, you stop practicing. Your mind starts to slip away. And once it goes, it usually doesn't come back.
<v Cecilia Brown>对于痴呆症，尤其如此。你越使用大脑，使用词汇，那些东西就越会保留在你身上。但当你被隔离时，你就停止了练习。你的思维开始衰退。一旦它走了，通常就不会再回来了。

00:26:57.350 --> 00:27:11.450
<v Cecilia Brown>In those first calls, I didn't worry too much about that with Dee. She had worked as a therapist for decades and would still kind of do informal therapy on everyone, including me. Without my asking, she'd just start working out my options with me on the phone.
<v Cecilia Brown>在最初的几次通话中，我没太担心迪的情况。她做过几十年的治疗师，仍然会以某种方式对每个人进行非正式的心理辅导，包括我。没等我要求，她就开始在电话里帮我理清选择。

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:22.640
<v Dee Brown>OK, what you do is, you get a big piece of paper, and you write down every one of them that you'd like to do. What do I want? What do I not want?
<v Dee Brown>好的，你该做的是，找一张大纸，写下所有你愿意做的事情。我想要什么？我不想要什么？

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:26.570
<v Cecilia Brown>I'd just finished grad school and wasn't really sure what to do with my life.
<v Cecilia Brown>我刚研究生毕业，真的不知道拿我的人生怎么办。

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:31.180
<v Dee Brown>See if you can find where you want to be and what you want to do when you grow up. I hate that one.
<v Dee Brown>看看你能找到你想在哪里，以及当你长大后想做什么。我讨厌那个词。

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:32.600
<v Dee Brown>[LAUGHTER]
<v Dee Brown>[笑声]

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:34.190
<v Dee Brown>It's fun. That make sense?
<v Dee Brown>这很有趣。说得通吗？

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:40.220
<v Cecilia Brown>Just like that. Yeah. Yeah, I just have to just figure out what I want.
<v Cecilia Brown>就像那样。是的。是的，我只需要找出我想要什么。

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:47.750
<v Dee Brown>Yeah, what do you want? You find out what you want to, and then you keep in touch. Because I love talking to you.
<v Dee Brown>是啊，你想要什么？你找出你想要的东西，然后保持联络。因为我喜欢和你说话。

00:27:47.750 --> 00:27:55.880
<v Cecilia Brown>I love talking to you too.
<v Cecilia Brown>我也喜欢和你说话。

00:27:55.880 --> 00:28:11.420
<v Ira Glass>When I called her the next week, April 5, she was still joking about things. But 11 days after that, my third call with her, she seemed much worse. She'd always liked her apartment before, but now I got the feeling it was starting to feel oppressive.
<v Ira Glass>下周，4月5日，我给她打电话时，她还在开玩笑。但11天后，我和她的第三次通话，她看起来糟糕多了。她以前一直喜欢她的公寓，但现在我觉得那种压抑感开始显现了。

00:28:11.420 --> 00:28:13.690
<v Cecilia Brown>I mean, we've got some sunshine today.
<v Cecilia Brown>我是说，今天阳光不错。

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:33.740
<v Dee Brown>Uh, I guess we-- we didn't get sunshine. Well, maybe a little. Oh, it's that little dribble through that little crack in the window. It's just a little tiny crack of light, not much to pay attention to.
<v Dee Brown>呃，我想我们——我们没得到阳光。好吧，也许有一点。哦，那是透过窗户缝透进来的一点点光。那只是一小道光，不值得注意。

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:57.720
<v Dee Brown>And my mouth is so dry. And the air is so dry. And my whole body is going, ugh. I'm just antsy, awful. I'm really just stuck here, it seems like.
<v Dee Brown>我的嘴太干了。空气也太干了。我整个身体都在说，额。我只是坐立不安，糟透了。我似乎真的被困在这里了。

00:28:57.720 --> 00:29:14.640
<v Cecilia Brown>Two weeks after that call, Dee started needing round-the-clock care. And by May, it was clear to me parts of Dee were drifting away. Things she'd explained to me weeks ago no longer made any sense to her, like they were swirling together in her head, and I had to explain them back to her.
<v Cecilia Brown>那次通话两周后，迪开始需要24小时照护。到五月，我清楚地感觉到迪的一部分正在漂离。几周前她向我解释过的事情对她来说已毫无意义，就像在她脑海里搅成了一团，我不得不反过来解释给她听。

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:29.850
<v Dee Brown>I'm trying to find out where my mind went sideways or something. I know where I am. It's just nothing anybody's saying is making any sense.
<v Dee Brown>我想找出我的思想是从哪里出了偏差。我知道我在哪里。只是别人说的话都没什么意义。

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:40.720
<v Dee Brown>And I can see some people walking along. And they're going up and down. And I don't know who's what or why.
<v Dee Brown>我可以看见有些人走来走去。他们走上走下。我不知道谁是谁，为什么。

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:54.540
<v Cecilia Brown>Yeah. Yeah, I mean, I think one of the things that is happening is that, because people are stuck in their apartments, they're walking up and down the hallway because it's the only place they can go to get exercise.
<v Cecilia Brown>是啊。是啊，我是说，我觉得发生的一件事是，因为人们被困在公寓里，他们会在走廊走上走下，因为那是他们唯一能去锻炼的地方。

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:55.950
<v Dee Brown>Yeah.
<v Dee Brown>是的。

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:59.650
<v Cecilia Brown>I think that's probably what's happening. That's why it seems so weird.
<v Cecilia Brown>我想那可能就是正在发生的事。这就是为什么它看起来那么奇怪。

00:29:59.650 --> 00:30:11.670
<v Dee Brown>It does. Everything feels weird. And I don't know. I'm just going to try and get some orientation or something.
<v Dee Brown>是的。一切都感觉很奇怪。我不知道。我只是试图找回方向什么的。

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:13.150
<v Cecilia Brown>Hm.
<v Cecilia Brown>嗯。

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:20.960
<v Dee Brown>And I have a television thing up in front of me, but it doesn't make any sense.
<v Dee Brown>我面前有一个电视，但它没什么意义。

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:24.850
<v Cecilia Brown>Hm. What do you mean? Like the things that people are saying doesn't make sense?
<v Cecilia Brown>嗯。你是什么意思？就像人们说的话没意义？

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:36.920
<v Dee Brown>Yeah, the people in there. And I don't know. It just seems like I'm in a strange world. But I will work on it.
<v Dee Brown>是的，里面的人。我不知道。这就像我处在一个奇怪的世界里。但我会努力解决的。

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:39.730
<v Cecilia Brown>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Cecilia Brown>是啊。是啊。

00:30:39.730 --> 00:30:57.530
<v Ira Glass>I will work on it-- that's so Dee, always sure there's a way to fix things if you just organized your thoughts correctly, even if the things she's trying to organize is her own dementia. I hated that she had to work this out alone, so I tried to work on it too. But I was kind of grasping at how to help.
<v Ira Glass>我会努力解决的——这太像迪了，总是坚信只要理清思路就能解决问题，即便她试图组织的东西是她自己的痴呆症。我恨她不得不独自面对这一切，所以我试图帮她。但我有点不知如何下手。

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:11.830
<v Cecilia Brown>OK, well, let's talk about something that might feel grounding. Why don't we talk about when you were living in Kansas City?
<v Cecilia Brown>好的，我们谈谈一些可能让你感到踏实的事情吧。我们谈谈你住在堪萨斯城的时候好吗？

00:31:11.830 --> 00:31:15.150
<v Dee Brown>Oh, boy, that goes way back.
<v Dee Brown>哦，孩子，那太久远了。

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:19.240
<v Cecilia Brown>Yeah, will you tell me about your house in Kansas City?
<v Cecilia Brown>是啊，你能跟我讲讲你在堪萨斯城的房子吗？

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:40.700
<v Dee Brown>Oh, I don't know if I can remember much about it. Oh, god, I just really feel like I don't know what they're expecting of me here. I don't know. I'm just off-center. That's all there is to it.
<v Dee Brown>哦，我不知道我还记不记得多少。哦，天哪，我真觉得我不知道他们在这里对我有什么期望。我不知道。我只是偏离了中心。这就是全部。

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:54.980
<v Cecilia Brown>It was so hard to comfort her. And it wasn't just her confusion. It was also that, the worse she got, the more physically uncomfortable she got too. There were basic things she needed that would have been so easy to do in person, but not over the phone.
<v Cecilia Brown>安慰她太难了。不仅仅是她的困惑。也是因为，她状况越糟，身体就越难受。她需要一些基本的东西，如果面对面，这些事做起来很容易，但在电话里不行。

00:31:54.980 --> 00:32:02.960
<v Dee Brown>My mouth is so dry that if I try and move my lips, it's just hurtful.
<v Dee Brown>我的嘴太干了，如果我试图动动嘴唇，那真的很痛。

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:04.190
<v Cecilia Brown>It hurts?
<v Cecilia Brown>很痛吗？

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:05.530
<v Dee Brown>Yeah.
<v Dee Brown>是的。

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:11.120
<v Cecilia Brown>Yeah. What about water? Do you have some water you could have a sip of?
<v Cecilia Brown>是啊。水呢？你有能喝一口的水吗？

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:36.640
<v Dee Brown>I have some water right here. I've gotten a sip of water, but-- oh. Now I can't get any water through the straw. Huh. I can't get any water to come through this straw. Oh, boy.
<v Dee Brown>我这儿有水。我喝了一口水，但是——哦。现在我没法通过吸管喝水了。哈。我没法通过这吸管喝到水。哦，天哪。

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:38.860
<v Cecilia Brown>Are you sucking?
<v Cecilia Brown>你在吸吗？

