<v Dee Brown>Yeah, trying to. Mm, mm! [GULPING] Finally got a little water.
<v Dee Brown>是啊，在试。嗯，嗯！[大口吞咽声]终于喝到一点水了。

00:32:49.010 --> 00:33:18.870
<v Cecilia Brown>Around this time, my family started talking about Dee's progression. Even though it should have been obvious to me what was happening, I didn't want to believe it. When I talked to my sister, we'd make excuses for Dee, say she was just having a bad day, or that it was her medication, which was always changing. We wanted to believe the old Dee would come back. My dad didn't think she would.
<v Cecilia Brown>大约在这个时候，我的家人开始讨论迪的进展。尽管对我来说这应该是显而易见的，但我不想相信。当我和我妹妹交谈时，我们会为迪找借口，说她只是今天状态不好，或者是因为她的药物，那些药物一直在变。我们想相信以前的迪会回来。但我爸觉得不会。

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:48.010
<v Cecilia Brown>By a few months into the pandemic, the toll of isolation on people with dementia was clear. Medical examiners started listing social isolation related to COVID-19 restrictions as a cause of death for people in long-term care centers. Someone from the Alzheimer's Association told me people with dementia have been wandering more, that maybe they're looking for the people who no longer visit. Dee started doing this, too, opening people's doors in the middle of the night.
<v Cecilia Brown>疫情几个月后，隔离对痴呆症患者的损害很明显了。法医开始将与新冠限制相关的社交隔离列为长期护理中心患者的死因。阿尔茨海默病协会的人告诉我，痴呆症患者四处游荡的情况增加了，也许他们在寻找那些不再来看他们的人。迪也开始这样做了，半夜打开别人的门。

