00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:20.410
<v Dee Brown>Uh-huh. Oh, and she's-- she's off somewhere, right?
<v Dee Brown>嗯——哦，而且她——她去哪儿了，对吧？

00:34:20.410 --> 00:34:54.830
<v Cecilia Brown>I thought a lot about whether it's OK to play these recordings on the radio. Talked about it with my family. Back when she was with it, Dee knew I might make a story out of these calls, and knew she had dementia, and was game to record. And I'm sure she'd want me to tell the true version of her story. That's Dee. By June, COVID death rates were starting to dip, and people were starting to meet up outside, emerge in small ways. Dee, though, felt almost completely out of reach.
<v Cecilia Brown>我想了很多关于在广播里播放这些录音是否合适。我和家人讨论过。当她还清醒时，迪知道我可能会把这些通话写成故事，知道她有痴呆症，并乐意录音。我相信她会希望我讲述她真实的生命故事。那就是迪。到了六月，新冠死亡率开始下降，人们开始在户外会面，以小规模出现。迪，虽然，感觉几乎完全无法触及。

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:58.820
<v Dee Brown>What would you like to know that I might know?
<v Dee Brown>你想知道些什么，也许我可能知道的？

00:34:58.820 --> 00:35:04.160
<v Cecilia Brown>Oh, nothing. I just wanted to talk to you.
