<v Ira Glass>Cecilia Brown. She's a documentary filmmaker in Portland, Oregon. Coming up, a vast theater with a daily show, but there's a catch. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues
<v Ira Glass>塞西莉亚·布朗。她是俄勒冈州波特兰的一位纪录片制片人。接下来的节目，一个拥有每日演出的巨大剧场，但有个前提。离节目继续还有一分钟，来自芝加哥公共广播电台。

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:57.620
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our show, "The Daily," stories about the things that we do every single day and how their "everydayness" is what's important about them and can reveal things that we might not notice otherwise. We have arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three, "It Had to be YouTube."
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目《每日》，讲述我们每天做的那些事，以及它们的“日常性”为何重要，并揭示了我们否则可能不会注意到的事物。我们已经进入了节目的第三幕。第三幕，“非YouTube不可。”

00:40:57.620 --> 00:41:16.080
<v Ira Glass>So Johnny Dark is somebody with a very specific, very unusual, labor intensive practice that he does everyday, more or less. Each day, he shoots and posts a video on YouTube. Kelefa Sanneh writes a lot about music and about other things for The New Yorker and watched a bunch of them.
<v Ira Glass>所以，约翰尼·达克（Johnny Dark）是一个有着非常具体、非常不寻常、劳动密集型习惯的人，他每天都会进行这种练习，或多或少。每一天，他都会拍摄并发布一段视频到YouTube上。凯莱法·桑内（Kelefa Sanneh）为《纽约客》撰写大量关于音乐和其他事物的文章，他观看了其中的许多内容。

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:35.220
<v Kelefa Sanneh>The first thing I noticed about Johnny Dark's YouTube page is that it goes on forever, an endless scrolling grid of thumbnail images, the same face, row after row, an older guy with an unnaturally brown pompadour. There are hundreds and hundreds of videos, going back more than five years. Each one is Johnny Dark singing a song.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>我注意到约翰尼·达克YouTube页面的第一件事就是它没完没了，一个无尽滚动网格的缩略图，同一张脸，一行接一行，一个留着不自然棕色蓬巴杜发型的老家伙。有成百上千个视频，追溯到五年多前。每一个都是约翰尼·达克在唱歌。

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:54.570
<v Kelefa Sanneh>And for all that work, all that music, there's no one watching. You can see it in the view counts-- 10 views, 23 views, seven views. I almost felt guilty clicking on them, like, by increasing the numbers, I was spoiling this pristine place, leaving footprints in practically unfootprinted terrain.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>而对于所有这些工作，所有这些音乐，没有人观看。你可以从观看次数中看出来——10次、23次、7次。点击它们时我几乎感到内疚，就像通过增加这些数字，我在破坏这个原始的地方，在几乎未被踏足的土地上留下了足迹。

00:41:54.570 --> 00:42:30.180
<v Kelefa Sanneh>I only ended up there because an old friend of Johnny's told me about it. He knew I had a taste for stubborn visionaries, for bodies of work that defy easy explanation. There's a simple formula to each video. Johnny Dark is all by himself, no instruments, nothing to sing along with, looking into the camera, dressed in a tuxedo, smiling, as if he were working a casino or a cruise ship, except he's standing in what might be a living room, though it might also be an antique shop. It's full of framed pictures and little statues. Often he's singing a classic, maybe a Sinatra song.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>我最终到那儿只是因为约翰尼的一位老朋友告诉了我。他知道我喜欢顽固的远见卓识者，喜欢那种难以轻易解释的作品集。每个视频都有一个简单的公式。约翰尼·达克独自一人，没有乐器，没有伴奏，注视着摄像机，穿着燕尾服，微笑，好像他在赌场或游轮上工作，但他却站在一个看起来可能是客厅的地方，尽管那也可能是个古董店。它摆满了带框的照片和小雕像。他经常唱经典歌曲，也许是弗兰克·辛纳屈（Sinatra）的歌。

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:56.290
<v Johnny Dark>(SINGING) I've got the world on a string, sitting on a rainbow. I've got the string around my finger. What a world. What a life. I'm in love.
<v Johnny Dark>(歌唱) 我掌握着世界的弦，坐在彩虹上。我的手指绕着这根弦。多么世界。多么生命。我坠入爱河。

00:42:56.290 --> 00:43:02.140
<v Kelefa Sanneh>Sometimes he'll do something that swings, like "Mack the Knife," but it's a lot of ballads. Hank Williams.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>有时他会做一些摇摆的曲子，比如《刀锋麦克》（Mack the Knife），但大多数是民谣。汉克·威廉姆斯（Hank Williams）。

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:10.630
<v Johnny Dark>(SINGING) Did you hear that lonesome whippoorwill?
<v Johnny Dark>(歌唱) 你听到那孤独的夜鹰了吗？

00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:12.430
<v Kelefa Sanneh>He went through a Carpenters phase.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>他经历过一个“木匠兄妹”（Carpenters）时期。

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:18.070
<v Johnny Dark>(SINGING) Why do birds suddenly appear--
<v Johnny Dark>(歌唱) 为什么鸟儿突然出现——

00:43:18.070 --> 00:43:32.860
<v Kelefa Sanneh>In the most recent videos, he's not wearing a tuxedo anymore. It's even more surreal. Now he's tightly framed in a shadowy room, a floating head singing into a mirror, still smiling, but now his smile seems kind of intense.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>在最新的视频中，他不再穿燕尾服了。甚至更加超现实。现在他被严密地框在阴暗的房间里，一个漂浮的头对着镜子歌唱，依然微笑着，但现在他的微笑看起来有点紧张。

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:48.100
<v Kelefa Sanneh>And he's dressed in dark colors, often black. You can see his hands in black gloves, holding the iPhone that's recording the video. The scene looks less like a cruise ship and more like a seance, which makes the song sound that much spookier, especially the sad ones.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>他穿着深色衣服，通常是黑色。你可以看到他戴着黑色手套的手，拿着录制视频的iPhone。这个场景看起来不像是在游轮上，更像是在招魂，这让那首歌听起来更诡异，尤其是那些悲伤的曲子。

00:43:48.100 --> 00:44:02.530
<v Johnny Dark>(SINGING) If a picture paints a thousand words, then why can't I paint you?
<v Johnny Dark>(歌唱) 如果画能描绘千言万语，那么为什么我画不出你？

00:44:02.530 --> 00:44:20.500
<v Kelefa Sanneh>The whole project seemed obsessive and haunted, as if some club had shut down in the 1970s, but the performance had kept going. Who was this for? Why do this? Why wake up every morning, get dressed, do your hair, and send another video out into the world, out into the void?
<v Kelefa Sanneh>整个项目看起来既执着又诡异，好像某个俱乐部在70年代就倒闭了，但表演还在继续。这是给谁看的？为什么要这么做？为什么要每天早上醒来，穿好衣服，梳好头发，把另一段视频传到世界上，传到虚无中？

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:30.190
<v Kelefa Sanneh>So I called him up. And I was nervous, to be honest. The videos seemed so compulsive, they made me wonder about his state of mind. But he was friendly, happy to explain.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>所以我给他打了电话。说实话，我很紧张。这些视频看起来太像强迫行为了，让我怀疑他的精神状态。但他很友好，很高兴解释。

00:44:30.190 --> 00:44:33.350
<v Kelefa Sanneh>What is that room that you're in, in those videos?
<v Kelefa Sanneh>你视频里的那个房间是什么？

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:35.460
<v Johnny Dark>That's actually my living room.
<v Johnny Dark>那其实是我的客厅。

00:44:35.460 --> 00:44:37.810
<v Kelefa Sanneh>OK. And there's all these statues and things.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>好的。那儿有很多雕像之类的东西。

00:44:37.810 --> 00:44:43.840
<v Johnny Dark>Yeah, statues and stuff. That's sort of what I like, you know? Nothing is real expensive.
<v Johnny Dark>是的，雕像之类的。那是我喜欢的，你知道吗？没什么真正昂贵的。

00:44:43.840 --> 00:44:44.950
<v Kelefa Sanneh>It looks expensive.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>看起来很贵。

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:50.380
<v Johnny Dark>Yeah, it does. It does. I always modeled my place after Buckingham Palace.
<v Johnny Dark>是的，确实。确实。我总是按照白金汉宫的样子布置我的地方。

00:44:50.380 --> 00:44:52.360
<v Kelefa Sanneh>You were dressed, what, in a tuxedo?
<v Kelefa Sanneh>你穿的是，什么，燕尾服？

00:44:52.360 --> 00:45:08.050
<v Johnny Dark>Yeah, the tuxedo was just a way of giving respect to what I thought the older folks would like to see. It's like wearing a tuxedo, working in Las Vegas, when you're opening for Tom Jones. It was just a way of giving respect.
<v Johnny Dark>是的，燕尾服只是为了向我认为老人家喜欢看的东西致敬。就像在拉斯维加斯做开场，给汤姆·琼斯（Tom Jones）暖场时穿燕尾服一样。那只是致敬的方式。

00:45:08.050 --> 00:45:38.350
<v Kelefa Sanneh>Johnny Dark has never actually opened for Tom Jones, the hit-making hunk who sang "It's Not Unusual." But he has worked with lots of other legendary acts. Long before he was singing on YouTube, Johnny Dark was performing for real audiences on stage and on TV, a busy actor and comedian. And so watching those videos, it's kind of like being visited by the ghost of showbusiness past, specifically Johnny's showbusiness past. He kept dropping all these names, household names, many of which weren't quite as household as they used to be.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>约翰尼·达克从未真正给汤姆·琼斯暖过场，那位唱《It's Not Unusual》的当红歌手。但他与许多其他传奇人物合作过。在他YouTube唱歌很久之前，约翰尼·达克就真正在舞台上和电视上为观众表演过，是一位忙碌的演员和喜剧演员。所以看那些视频，就像被演艺界的幽灵造访了一样，特别是约翰尼曾经的演艺界。他不断抛出所有这些名字，家喻户晓的名字，其中许多名字不如过去那么家喻户晓了。

00:45:38.350 --> 00:45:39.100
<v Johnny Dark>Tony Bennett--
<v Johnny Dark>托尼·班内特（Tony Bennett）——

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:47.170
<v Kelefa Sanneh>In the course of a two-hour conversation, he told me about shining Tony Bennett's shoes in Wildwood, New Jersey, being best friends with Fabian, the '50s heartthrob.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>在两个小时的交谈中，他告诉我他在新泽西州怀尔德伍德擦托尼·班内特的鞋子，与50年代万人迷法比安（Fabian）是最好的朋友。

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:48.310
<v Johnny Dark>My best friend Fabian.
<v Johnny Dark>我最好的朋友法比安。

00:45:48.310 --> 00:45:58.450
<v Kelefa Sanneh>He toured with Ginger Rogers and also with the Righteous Brothers. At one point, he found himself up against Michael Keaton. They were both competing for a spot on Donnie and Marie Osmond's variety show.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>他与琴吉·罗杰斯（Ginger Rogers）一起巡演，也与正义兄弟（Righteous Brothers）一起巡演。一度，他发现自己不得不与迈克尔·基顿（Michael Keaton）竞争。他们都在竞争唐尼和玛丽·奥斯蒙德（Donnie and Marie Osmond）综艺节目的位置。

00:45:58.450 --> 00:46:05.600
<v Johnny Dark>And I got the Donnie and Marie show! And I don't know whatever happened to Michael Keaton.
<v Johnny Dark>我得到了唐尼和玛丽的节目！我不知道迈克尔·基顿后来怎么了。

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:25.060
<v Kelefa Sanneh>A spot on a variety show, that was kind of the perfect gig for a guy like Johnny Dark. He's an entertainer, which is the job that maybe doesn't even exist anymore. It means you get up in front of an audience and do whatever it takes to keep them happy-- songs, jokes, skits, dancing, impressions. Here he is on TV in the 1970s.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>综艺节目的位置，那对约翰尼·达克这样的家伙来说是完美的演出。他是一个艺人，这种工作也许已经不存在了。这意味着你站在观众面前，不惜一切代价让他们高兴——歌曲、笑话、短剧、舞蹈、模仿。这是他在70年代电视上的样子。

00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:26.310
<v Kelefa Sanneh>[APPLAUSE]
<v Kelefa Sanneh>[掌声]

00:46:26.310 --> 00:46:35.360
<v Johnny Dark>Ladies and gentlemen, I'd like you give me my impression of a rodeo auctioneer. Imagine this guy coming home to his wife at night. Hi, honey, how about a kiss? Another kiss.
<v Johnny Dark>女士们先生们，我想给你们表演一段牛仔拍卖员的模仿。想象一下这个家伙晚上回家见妻子。嗨，亲爱的，吻一个怎么样？再吻一个。

00:46:35.360 --> 00:46:40.730
<v Johnny Dark>Make that three kisses on the right cheek by the little lady with the checkered apron. What's for dinner? I said, what's for dinner? I hear hot dogs.
<v Johnny Dark>让那个系着格子围裙的小女士在右脸颊吻三下。晚饭吃什么？我说，晚饭吃什么？我听到热狗。

00:46:40.730 --> 00:46:48.370
<v Johnny Dark>I hear hot dogs and beans. Do I hear baked beans or lima beans? I hear baked beans. Make that baked beans and butter. Make that Boston baked beans and butter. Do I hear bread? I hear brown bread.
<v Johnny Dark>我听到热狗和豆子。我听到烘豆还是利马豆？我听到烘豆。让那是烘豆和黄油。让那是波士顿烘豆和黄油。我听到面包吗？我听到棕色面包。

00:46:48.370 --> 00:47:23.870
<v Kelefa Sanneh>But by this point, in the 1970s, entertainers in tuxedos were going extinct. And Johnny Dark had that old school style, even though his career really began in a club where a new sensibility was emerging-- the Comedy Store in LA. The regulars there were not all purpose old school entertainers. They were performers with a point of view, unpredictable stand-up comics like Richard Pryor, Robin Williams, Andy Kaufman, but also a young David Letterman and Jay Leno. Johnny Dark got a job there as an MC, introducing the comics. He'd open with a song. And eventually, he started telling jokes, too.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>但到了70年代这个点，穿燕尾服的艺人正在灭绝。约翰尼·达克有那种老派风格，尽管他的职业生涯真正始于一个新兴感性出现的俱乐部——洛杉矶的喜剧商店（Comedy Store）。那里的常客并非都是全能的老派艺人。他们是有观点的表演者，不可预测的单口喜剧演员如理查德·普赖尔（Richard Pryor）、罗宾·威廉斯（Robin Williams）、安迪·考夫曼（Andy Kaufman），还有年轻的大卫·莱特曼（David Letterman）和杰·雷诺（Jay Leno）。约翰尼·达克在那里找到了一份主持的工作，介绍喜剧演员。他会以一首歌开场。最终，他也开始讲笑话了。

00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:34.220
<v Johnny Dark>We were all part of one peer group. But we weren't all geniuses, you know? Robin Williams and people of that genre, how could I compete with those guys? They were geniuses.
<v Johnny Dark>我们都是同一个同伴群体的一部分。但我们并不都是天才，你知道吗？罗宾·威廉斯和那个流派的人，我怎么能和那些家伙竞争？他们是天才。

00:47:34.220 --> 00:47:51.980
<v Johnny Dark>I mean, Robin Williams, I used to pick him up in front of Chase's to drive him out to the Newport Beach Laff Stop. And I was the closing act. Well, he would get up and, in the middle, do 30, 40 minutes. It was killer stuff! I couldn't compete with that.
<v Johnny Dark>我是说，罗宾·威廉斯，我过去常在蔡斯（Chase's）门前接他，开车送他去纽波特海滩的Laff Stop。我是压轴。好吧，他会上去，在中间做30、40分钟。那真是杀手级的东西！我没法和那个竞争。

00:47:51.980 --> 00:48:06.590
<v Kelefa Sanneh>It's true. I'm pretty sure you've heard of Robin Williams, and you've probably never heard of Johnny Dark. But he built a successful career, had small roles on a bunch of popular sitcoms, made good money on the road, supported a family. His most memorable role might be this one.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>这是真的。我很确定你听说过罗宾·威廉斯，而且你可能从没听说过约翰尼·达克。但他建立了一个成功的职业生涯，在许多受欢迎的情景喜剧中扮演小角色，在巡回演出中赚了不错的钱，养活了一个家庭。他最令人难忘的角色可能是这个。

00:48:06.590 --> 00:48:12.050
<v David Letterman>Ladies and gentlemen, please say hello to Johnny, the world's oldest page, I think.
<v David Letterman>女士们先生们，请向约翰尼问好，我想是世界上最老的通讯员。

00:48:12.050 --> 00:48:38.390
<v Kelefa Sanneh>In the 2000s, David Letterman pulled his old friend Johnny Dark into the spotlight for a series of guest appearances on The Late Show, giving Johnny a little bit of the fame and recognition he had always wanted. The bit was that he was the world's oldest CBS page, an intern, basically, who'd been killing time in the bowels of the Ed Sullivan Theater for 38 years. The character was crappy and defiant. He had no interest in the show and mainly wanted Letterman, the big-shot host, to leave him alone.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>2000年代，大卫·莱特曼将他的老朋友约翰尼·达克拉入了聚光灯下，在《深夜秀》中多次客串，给了约翰尼一点他一直渴望的名声和认可。这个桥段是他是一个世界上最老的CBS通讯员，基本上是个实习生，已经在埃德·沙利文剧院的深处浪费了38年时间。这个角色很烂且叛逆。他对节目没兴趣，主要想让大卫·莱特曼这个大牌主持离他远点。

00:48:38.390 --> 00:48:42.830
<v David Letterman>Oh, I'm sorry, Johnny, you can't-- you're not allowed to smoke in the theater. I'm sorry.
<v David Letterman>哦，抱歉，约翰尼，你不能——你不被允许在剧院抽烟。抱歉。

00:48:42.830 --> 00:48:47.200
<v Johnny Dark>Well, remind me to bring that up next time you blow smoke up a celebrity's ass.
<v Johnny Dark>好吧，提醒我下次你吹捧名人的时候带上这个。

00:48:47.200 --> 00:48:51.440
<v Johnny Dark>[APPLAUSE]
<v Johnny Dark>[掌声]

00:48:51.440 --> 00:49:16.910
<v Kelefa Sanneh>Before I talked to him, I sort of assumed that Johnny Dark was going to be a lot like that character, that he'd be bitter about his long and miscellaneous career, about never achieving real stardom, like maybe it had driven him crazy, and now he was sitting at home, compulsively making these videos every day, creating his own lonely, private Hollywood. But he said it's not like that. He told me over and over again in different ways that he wasn't bitter.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>在我跟他谈话之前，我假设约翰尼·达克会很像那个角色，他会对他漫长而杂乱的职业生涯感到痛苦，因为从未获得真正的明星地位，就像也许它让他疯了，现在他坐在家里，强迫性地每天做这些视频，创造他自己孤独的、私密的“好莱坞”。但他表示不是那样。他一遍又一遍地以不同的方式告诉我他并不痛苦。

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:21.710
<v Johnny Dark>No, no, I was grateful. I was thrilled to be with David.
<v Johnny Dark>不，不，我很感激。能和大卫在一起我很激动。

00:49:21.710 --> 00:49:35.360
<v Kelefa Sanneh>He told me he was glad to have had the chance to entertain so many people. He said he was happy. He's 80 years old, living in LA in a house full of little statues, spending time with his grandchildren everyday, wandering around the park, cooking them dinner.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>他告诉我他很高兴有机会招待那么多人。他说他很快乐。他80岁了，住在洛杉矶一间摆满小雕像的房子里，每天陪孙子孙女，在公园闲逛，为他们做晚饭。

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:36.560
<v Johnny Dark>Making pizza for them.
<v Johnny Dark>给他们做披萨。

00:49:36.560 --> 00:49:42.320
<v Kelefa Sanneh>There was one moment, though, when he finally admitted that it has been frustrating sometimes.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>不过，有一个时刻，他终于承认有时确实感到沮丧。

00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:59.630
<v Johnny Dark>I feel sort of a love-hate relationship with showbusiness in the fact that I've been close to so many big things that didn't come true. It couldn't come through. It's really tough.
<v Johnny Dark>我对演艺界有一种又爱又恨的感觉，因为我离那么多没成真大事太近了。它没能成功。这真的很艰难。

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:05.600
<v Johnny Dark>Showbusiness is tough. But it's fun initially, and then it gets tough. And it gets tougher.
<v Johnny Dark>演艺界很艰难。但起初很有趣，然后就变得艰难了。而且它变得越来越艰难。

00:50:05.600 --> 00:50:20.000
<v Johnny Dark>I wouldn't want my grandkids in showbusiness. I really wouldn't. I mean, I was born to it. But if you can do anything else in the world, do that. Don't get in showbusiness.
<v Johnny Dark>我不希望我的孙子孙女进入演艺界。我真不希望。我是说，我生来就属于它。但如果世界上你能做别的任何事，就去做那件事。别进演艺界。

00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:24.430
<v Kelefa Sanneh>So you're saying you didn't have a choice but to do this. Why? Just because you loved it too much?
<v Kelefa Sanneh>所以你的意思是，你别无选择只能做这个。为什么？只是因为你太爱它了？

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:40.150
<v Johnny Dark>It was in my heart, just like my wife. When I met my wife, I had no choice. I didn't want to get married. But that's what love is. Love doesn't give you a choice, I don't think. And neither does showbusiness.
<v Johnny Dark>它在我的心里，就像我妻子一样。当我遇到我妻子时，我别无选择。我不想结婚。但那就是爱。爱不会给你选择，我不认为。演艺界也不给。

00:50:40.150 --> 00:51:02.380
<v Johnny Dark>I was always fascinated by guys that were so smart, like Jerry Seinfeld and people like that that could put the show and the business together. I always had trouble with the business. Even now, everything that I do, over 1,000 songs I've sang on YouTube, will I be paid for that work? Will I be paid for that effort?
<v Johnny Dark>我一直着迷于那些非常聪明的家伙，比如杰瑞·宋飞（Jerry Seinfeld）那些能把表演和商业结合起来的人。我处理商业总是有困难。即便现在，我所做的一切，我在YouTube上唱了1000多首歌，我会为那份工作获得报酬吗？我会为那份努力获得报酬吗？

00:51:02.380 --> 00:51:06.450
<v Johnny Dark>No. Why do I do it? Because I have no choice.
<v Johnny Dark>不。我为什么这么做？因为我别无选择。

00:51:06.450 --> 00:51:13.840
<v Kelefa Sanneh>The word "entertainer" sounds maybe kind of casual. You do a little of this, a little of that. Do it well enough, everyone's happy.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>“艺人”这个词听起来也许有点随意。你做一点这个，一点那个。做得足够好，每个人都高兴。

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:23.710
<v Kelefa Sanneh>But Johnny Dark is not a casual guy. And he's definitely not a casual singer. Thanks to these videos, his life is nonstop rehearsals, nonstop singing.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>但约翰尼·达克不是一个随意的家伙。他也绝对不是一个随意的歌手。多亏了这些视频，他的生活是不间断的排练，不间断的歌唱。

00:51:23.710 --> 00:51:34.240
<v Johnny Dark>I made it sort of a discipline thing for me. I have to know exactly every line every single day. So every morning, I get up, and it's just like I'm taking a gig.
<v Johnny Dark>我把它变成了我的一门纪律。我每天都必须确切地知道每一行歌词。所以每天早上，我起床，感觉就像在接演出。

00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:39.610
<v Johnny Dark>I get in the shower. I go over the song. I listen to the song on tape while I'm shaving.
<v Johnny Dark>我在淋浴时排练。我刮胡子时听着歌带。

00:51:39.610 --> 00:52:01.270
<v Johnny Dark>But don't forget, I've listened to that song for 24 hours off and on with the kids. When I'm with the kids at the park, I sing the song aloud in a certain area where I don't bother anybody. But no, I rehearse all the time. I've got it down. So yesterday, me singing "Lollipops and Roses" didn't exist. Today it does exist.
<v Johnny Dark>但别忘了，我和孩子们断断续续地听了那首歌24小时。当我和孩子们在公园时，我在某个没人打扰的区域大声唱歌。但没有，我一直在排练。我练好了。所以昨天，我唱的《棒棒糖与玫瑰》不存在。今天它存在。

00:52:01.270 --> 00:52:19.270
<v Johnny Dark>(SINGING) Bring her nice things, sugar and spice things, roses and lollipops, and lollipops and roses.
<v Johnny Dark>(歌唱) 带给她好东西，糖和香料，玫瑰和棒棒糖，棒棒糖和玫瑰。

00:52:19.270 --> 00:52:28.270
<v Johnny Dark>Basically, I'm thinking about the song. I'm thinking about doing it right. I'm thinking about how I was terrible. I'm thinking about how that was good.
<v Johnny Dark>基本上，我在思考那首歌。我在思考如何把它唱好。我在想我当时表现有多糟。我在想那表现有多好。

00:52:28.270 --> 00:52:41.110
<v Johnny Dark>I'm just thinking about the art. And sometimes I'll do a song four or five times. I'll even post it on YouTube. And then I'll listen to it and I'll go, I don't hear it. I don't hear the music.
<v Johnny Dark>我只是在思考艺术。有时我会把一首歌唱四五遍。我甚至会发布到YouTube上。然后我听了它，我就想，我没听到。我没听到音乐。

00:52:41.110 --> 00:52:49.420
<v Johnny Dark>And I'll delete it. And then I'll do it again. And if I don't hear it, I'll delete that. And then I do it again, and if I hear it, then I'll post it.
<v Johnny Dark>然后我会删了它。然后再唱一次。如果我没听到，我会再删了它。然后我再唱一次，如果我听到了，那我就发布。

00:52:49.420 --> 00:52:57.010
<v Johnny Dark>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Johnny Dark>[音乐播放]

00:52:57.010 --> 00:53:02.810
<v Kelefa Sanneh>I mean, is there a sense in which these songs are more for you than for anyone else?
<v Kelefa Sanneh>我的意思是，这些歌是否更多是为你自己而不是为别人唱的？

00:53:02.810 --> 00:53:18.910
<v Johnny Dark>They're as much for me as for anyone else, yeah. They're as much for me because I want to keep this working as good as it could possibly work, you know? I meant my brain by that. I was pointing to my brain.
<v Johnny Dark>它们既是为我唱的，也是为别人唱的，是的。它们更多是为我唱的，因为我想让这个大脑尽可能好地运转，你知道吗？我指的是我的大脑。我刚才指着我的大脑。

00:53:18.910 --> 00:53:31.540
<v Kelefa Sanneh>He also posts the videos on Facebook. And he gets more engagement there-- not a massive amount, but 30 or 40 likes per video. And people show up in the comments section, reminiscing about the old songs they loved growing up.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>他也把视频发布到Facebook上。他在那里获得了更多的参与——不是海量，但每个视频有30或40个赞。人们在评论区出现，怀念他们成长过程中喜爱的老歌。

00:53:31.540 --> 00:53:48.940
<v Kelefa Sanneh>But Johnny still directs people to his YouTube page. He says that's his personal archive, the thing he's building. He wants to leave it behind for his grandchildren. I like to picture them decades from now, sitting around a laptop, listening to grandpa singing "Me and My Shadow" or "The Very Thought of You."
<v Kelefa Sanneh>但约翰尼仍然引导人们去他的YouTube页面。他说那是他的个人档案，他在构建的东西。他想把它留给孙子孙女。我喜欢想象几十年后他们坐在笔记本电脑旁，听爷爷唱《我和我的影子》或《非常思念你》。

00:53:48.940 --> 00:53:59.910
<v Kelefa Sanneh>But there's one last reason Johnny makes videos everyday. He still has showbiz ambitions. He's currently pitching a sitcom, in fact, with a friend of his. But on YouTube, he doesn't have to pitch.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>但约翰尼每天制作视频还有一个原因。他依然有演艺抱负。事实上，他目前正与一位朋友推销一个情景喜剧。但在YouTube上，他不需要推销。

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:09.240
<v Kelefa Sanneh>And I think that might be the main reason he's been doing it for so long. Maybe there aren't many people there watching. But at the same time, there's no one there to stop him.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>我认为这可能是他做这么久的主要原因。也许没多少人在看。但与此同时，也没人能阻止他。

00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:20.880
<v Johnny Dark>I think the fact that I could sing and not have an audience, and not depend upon anybody-- I don't have to rehearse for anybody. I don't have to ask anybody's permission. I can greenlight myself everyday.
<v Johnny Dark>我认为我能唱歌却没有观众，不依赖任何人的事实——我不必为任何人排练。我不必征求任何人的许可。我可以每天给自己开绿灯。

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:28.200
<v Johnny Dark>Do you know how hard that is to do after waiting for somebody to say, OK, we'll greenlight you. Let's go with Johnny Dark. OK, let's go with Johnny.
<v Johnny Dark>你知道在等待别人说，“好的，我们给你开绿灯。用约翰尼·达克吧。好的，选约翰尼。”之后，做到这一点有多难吗？

00:54:28.200 --> 00:54:56.190
<v Johnny Dark>Of all the things that I've done-- and there are tons of things-- there are tons of other things I haven't done, where you go, oh, I wish I had that part. I came close to-- and that's what singing is. So I keep my self-esteem. I don't have to say, well, you walked away from it, man. You walked away from it. Well, I didn't walk away from it. I may not be getting big bucks for it, but I didn't walk away from it, man. I'm entertaining everyday.
<v Johnny Dark>在我所做的所有事情中——那有成吨的事情——还有成吨我没做过的事情，你会想，哦，我希望我有那个部分。我差点就——这就是唱歌的意义。所以我保持了自尊。我不必说，好吧，你逃避了它，伙计。你逃避了它。好吧，我没逃避它，伙计。我可能没因此赚大钱，但我没逃避它，伙计。我每天都在娱乐。

00:54:56.190 --> 00:55:06.810
<v Johnny Dark>I didn't walk away from it. I'm in the business. My toes are in the water.
<v Johnny Dark>我没逃避它。我还在这一行里。我的脚趾还在水里。

00:55:06.810 --> 00:55:19.470
<v Kelefa Sanneh>You see Johnny smiling in the videos, eager to please. But underneath it, this whole project is kind of aggro, defiant. He's going to decide when it's over, and it's not over yet.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>你看到约翰尼在视频中微笑着，急于取悦。但在那之下，整个项目有一种攻击性，一种挑衅。他会决定什么时候结束，而现在还没结束。

00:55:19.470 --> 00:55:30.120
<v Kelefa Sanneh>Like, I don't need any of you. Sure, I want an audience, but I don't need one. I'm here to entertain. And I'm going to be here, whether you're entertained or not.
<v Kelefa Sanneh>就像，我不需要你们任何一个人。当然，我想要观众，但我不需要。我是来娱乐的。无论你们是否被娱乐，我都会在这儿。

00:55:30.120 --> 00:55:53.560
<v Johnny Dark>(SINGING) What a world. Man, this is the life. Hey, now, I'm in love.
<v Johnny Dark>(歌唱) 多么世界。伙计，这就是生活。嘿，现在，我坠入爱河。

00:55:53.560 --> 00:55:58.170
<v Ira Glass>Kelefa Sanneh, he's a staff writer for The New Yorker Magazine.
<v Ira Glass>凯莱法·桑内，他是《纽约客》杂志的特约撰稿人。

00:55:58.170 --> 00:56:10.500
<v Ira Glass>Act Four. So the title of today's episode, "The Daily," is also the title of a podcast. If you're hearing us, you've probably heard them too. And so with them in mind, we end today's show with this.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕。所以今天剧集的标题，“每日”，也是一个播客的标题。如果你能听到我们，你可能也听过他们。所以考虑到他们，我们以这个结束今天的节目。

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:12.290
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放]

00:56:12.290 --> 00:56:33.580
<v Ira Glass>Here's what else you need to know today. The atoms in your body replace themselves. So you are not the same person you were a year ago-- or anyway, around 98% of you is not the same person. The only atoms in our bodies that seem to stay with us our whole lives are buried in the DNA of some of the cells in our brain and our heart, and also in our bones and teeth.
<v Ira Glass>这就是今天你需要知道的其他事情。你体内的原子会自我更新。所以你不是一年前的那个你——或者说，大约98%的你不是同一个人。我们体内唯一似乎终身陪伴我们的原子埋藏在某些脑细胞和心脏细胞的DNA中，也埋藏在骨骼和牙齿中。

00:56:33.580 --> 00:56:59.940
<v Ira Glass>Here's what else you need to know today. Fruit Loops-- they're all the same flavor. The yellow ones, green ones, red ones-- all the same. Here's what else you need to know today. We spend 10 minutes a day looking for stuff we've lost. That's the average person on the average day, of course. The number comes from a survey of 3,000 adults. 14 people in the survey said the amount of time for them searching for lost items was over an hour a day.
<v Ira Glass>这是今天你需要知道的其他事情。水果圈麦片（Fruit Loops）——它们都是一样的味道。黄色的、绿色的、红色的——全是一样。这是今天你需要知道的其他事情。我们每天花10分钟寻找丢失的东西。当然，这是普通人平均每一天的数值。这个数字来自一项针对3000名成年人的调查。调查中有14人说他们每天寻找丢失物品的时间超过一小时。

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:17.910
<v Ira Glass>Here's what else you need to know today. 1 million species are on the verge of extinction. It's 1/4 of all mammals, 1/5 of all reptiles, 1/6 of all birds. So as we fix our hair and scroll through Twitter and try to figure out what movie we're going to watch next, that's still happening. Here's what else you need to know today.
<v Ira Glass>这是今天你需要知道的其他事情。100万个物种处于灭绝边缘。那是所有哺乳动物的1/4，所有爬行动物的1/5，所有鸟类的1/6。所以当我们整理发型、刷推特并试图弄清楚接下来看什么电影时，这一切仍在发生。这是今天你需要知道的其他事情。

00:57:17.910 --> 00:57:51.120
<v Ira Glass>And by "you" this time, I mean you. You need to know that last night, when you put that plate of food in front of me, it was hard not to stare at it like a character bonked on the head in a cartoon. An average Thursday, you take cabbage, onions, carrots, coconut milk-- I don't know what else-- the most ordinary stuff in the world and somehow give me something that is better than anything I could have imagined, like you always do with everything you do. Our life is so different from anything I thought would ever happen to me.
<v Ira Glass>这次说的“你”，我指的是你。你需要知道，昨天晚上，当你把那盘食物放在我面前时，我很难不盯着它看，就像卡通片里被敲了脑袋的角色一样。一个平常的周四，你拿卷心菜、洋葱、胡萝卜、椰奶——我不知道还有什么——世界上最普通的东西，却以某种方式给了我比我所能想象的任何东西都好，就像你做的每一件事一样。我们的生活与我曾想过会发生在我身上的任何事都太不同了。

00:57:51.120 --> 00:58:08.940
<v Ira Glass>Here's what else you need to know today. When I was a kid, at night, sometimes after dinner, my mom and I would sit in the kitchen and talk. And I don't remember about what, but I remember the feeling of it was just, we were really listening to each other and hearing each other in that way that doesn't always happen.
<v Ira Glass>这是今天你需要知道的其他事情。当我还是个孩子时，晚上，晚饭后，我和妈妈有时会坐在厨房聊天。我不记得聊的是什么，但我记得那种感觉，就是我们真的在倾听彼此，在那种并不总是发生的层面上理解彼此。

00:58:08.940 --> 00:58:26.070
<v Ira Glass>It felt close. In my memory, the room is dimly lit. Sometimes my dad would come to the door of the kitchen, lit from the hall light behind him, and see us there, talking quietly, and not join in, but just stand there, apart from us.
<v Ira Glass>感觉很亲近。在我的记忆里，房间光线昏暗。有时我爸爸会来到厨房门口，被身后大厅的光照亮，看着我们，在那儿安静地聊天，没有加入，只是站在那里，和我们保持距离。

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:34.260
<v Ira Glass>And I remember a little panic, like, oh, I hope he doesn't feel left out. I hope he doesn't feel jealous. I hope he won't get mad.
<v Ira Glass>我记得有一点点恐慌，就像，哦，我希望他不会感到被排斥。我希望他不会感到嫉妒。我希望他不会生气。

00:58:34.260 --> 00:59:20.110
<v Ira Glass>I was-- I don't know-- seven or eight, feeling this mix of protectiveness towards him and fear, which-- I don't know-- is funny to think about today. My mom, long dead, my dad, getting up there, needing help with stuff that he never needed help with before. All that's left of that feeling is the protectiveness part. The atoms of our bodies replace themselves so many times over that things between us have become very simple, which I suppose is the best case of what can happen between parents and kids. But it doesn't always feel that way.
<v Ira Glass>当时我——我不知道——七八岁，感受着这种对他的保护欲和恐惧，这——我不知道——今天想来很有趣。我妈早已去世，我爸，年纪大了，需要他在过去从未需要过帮助的事情上的帮助。那感觉剩下的唯一东西就是保护的那部分。我们身体的原子自我更新了太多次，以至于我们之间的事情变得非常简单，我想这可能是父母和孩子之间能发生的最好情况。但这并不总是那种感觉。

00:59:20.110 --> 00:59:51.470
